# Paths where agent autonomy is genuinely dangerous (§9.6).
#
# The workflow directory matters most: CI has access to deployment credentials, so
# an agent able to modify workflows unreviewed can exfiltrate them (G-048). The
# agent may still *propose* changes to any path here — it simply cannot merge them
# alone.
#
# §9.6 is explicit that "the agent never approves a PR that touches a CODEOWNERS
# path, including its own." Enable "require review from Code Owners" in branch
# protection so this is enforced by the platform rather than by the agent's
# restraint — a rule the constrained party can choose to follow is not a control.
#
# Everything not listed here: the agent may merge its own PR once CI,
# guardrails-check.sh, and the §3 invariant checks pass. The owner may tighten
# this to review-everything at any time; the point is that the setting is
# deliberate and written down, not discovered on the first blocked merge.

/.github/workflows/    @vppillai
/infrastructure/       @vppillai
/scripts/bootstrap*    @vppillai
/docs/security/        @vppillai

# Not in §9.6's list, added for the same reason the workflow directory is there:
# this image is what every job runs inside, so a change to it changes what every
# check executes. An unreviewed toolchain change can neutralise every gate at
# once while the pipeline still reports green.
/containers/           @vppillai

# CODEOWNERS protecting itself. Without this line, the first thing an
# unconstrained change could do is remove the constraints above.
/.github/CODEOWNERS    @vppillai
